## About this Devlog

This devlog contains reports about progress in the project. It does not cover
everything that has happened, but it's a heartbeat that shows how the project
progresses over time.

This page lists entries for the first quarter of 2026, for past and future entries consult the [devlog archive](/devlog/).

## Entries

### [Reaching NGI Zero Commons Fund Second Stage!]($section.id('2026-03-08T10:00:00').data('author','xq'))

While writing this devlog, i've been contacted by the [nlnet Foundation](https://nlnet.nl/)
informing me that i've **reached the second stage** of the selection for the 
[NGI Zero Commons Fund](https://nlnet.nl/commonsfund/)!

**This is really really great news!**

For those who didn't know:  
I've applied to the 2025 round of the fund back in [November 2025](/devlog/2025-q4#2025-11-26T00:00:00).

### [The Devlog]($section.id('2026-03-08T00:00:00').data('author','xq'))

I've finally started working on a thing that i wanted to have for so long now:

**A devlog**

The idea started at new years eve, where I decided that I want to use the
[newsletter](https://buttondown.com/ashet.computer) to inform the people interested
in the project that I'm alive, the project is alive and is performing well.

This yielded the idea of making a "2025 at Ashet Technologies" blog post or
newsletter email, and after scrolling through the whole
[Discord #showcase](https://discord.com/channels/1042072811897880576/1042076164442832926)
back until summer 2024, i've figured that this much content would never fit into
a basic newsletter.

This meant that i had to do the devlog and retro-actively backfill it until the
project launch.

This is only fair as [Discord had some bad press](https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvgv0yg4n9lo)
after a forceful introduction of age verification. This means that in the future, even fewer
people will join the [Ashet Technologies Research Center](https://discord.gg/TMa5FZTUdq).

To make the project updates available to everyone, I've now made this devlog and also
made an account on [Hackaday](https://hackaday.io/) where I'm trying to push more fine grained
updates than in the devlog itself.

*Enjoy reading!*

> []($block.attrs('note','throwback'))
> It was an incredible journey up till today, and I'm curious how the project
> will turn out in the future.

### [Selfhosted RISC-V Emulator]($section.id('2026-03-06T00:00:00').data('author','xq'))

I've got a week of Claude Pro sponsored by a friend and i've used it to fulfill me a long-standing dream of shipping our own emulator with Ashet OS.

It's still in a work branch, but the screenshot should explain basically everything.

The emulator is … surprisingly fast considering it's not optimized for performance, but for flexibility.

On my laptop i can achieve roughly 160 MIPS stable, which makes Ashet OS fairly usable.

We have a new port for the emulator supporting the minimum viable feature set, allowing introspection of how the OS is integrated into new hardware.

It's not merged yet into the master branch, but if you want to try it out today, you can check out work/rv32-emulator and build like this:

```
# build kernel:
$ zig build -Doptimize-kernel -Doptimize-apps=ReleaseSafe rv32-ashet-base tools
# build emulator:
$ (cd src/tools/emulator/ && zig build -Drelease)
# run emulator:
./src/tools/emulator/zig-out/bin/emulator ./zig-out/rv32-ashet-base/kernel.bin --disk0 ./zig-out/rv32-ashet-base/disk.img
```

This should then proceed to boot Ashet OS in an unpredictable time. You can play around with the speed slider, and if you hold ctrl and click it, you can actually enter any number you want (i use 150 to 200).


